# Product-docs house style

This repo holds Extole's customer-facing documentation, built on **Mintlify**. When you author or edit a page here (`guides/**`, `product/**`, `technical/**`, `news/**` — `.mdx`), apply Extole's documentation house style. The unambiguous fixes below are **not** optional polish to raise later: make them in the same edit, before human review, so reviewers spend their time on substance, not the same wording corrections every time.

Full term map, style reference, and authoring workflow: the **product-docs-authoring** skill ([`.agents/skills/product-docs-authoring/SKILL.md`](../skills/product-docs-authoring/SKILL.md)), its [`glossary.md`](../skills/product-docs-authoring/glossary.md), and [`style-guide.md`](../skills/product-docs-authoring/style-guide.md). The canonical human-owned sources are the **Extole Style Guide** and **Extole Content Strategy Outline** (Google Docs, docs-team owned); this rule is the agent-actionable distillation, not a replacement.

## Apply automatically (do not wait to be asked)

- **Terminology:** use the preferred term from the glossary. Most load-bearing for reporting/journey docs: in prose, a person completes an **event**, not a "step" (corpus favors "event" ~3.8:1) — but only in loose prose; keep "step" where it is a schema field (`step_name`, `stepName`) or the deliberate journey-structure unit. Other standing swaps: "landing page" (not "microsite"), "pop-up" (not "lightbox"), "outbound" (not "off-site"), "quality engine/rules" (not "fraud engine/rules"), "advocate"/"friend" for the two sides of a referral, and "distinct" (not "different") when separating two concepts.
- **Voice:** address the reader as "you"; use the **imperative mood** for how-to steps ("Select **Reports**", not "You would then select…"). Match the brand voice — professional, clear, client-centric — and avoid jargon where a plain-English label exists.
- **Cut hedging and filler.** Delete redundancy and padding: `just`, `simply`, `very`, `note that`, `please note`, `in order to` → `to`. "related but different concepts and should not be confused" → "related but distinct concepts". Prefer one precise word over a prepositional phrase.
- **Title Case** for page titles and section headings (observed corpus convention — 395/461 titles — and what reviewers use; it differs from the 2014 Google style-guide draft, so treat this as the current house rule). Body text uses normal sentence capitalization. American English spelling; imperial units unless the doc is for an international audience.
- **Navigation:** bold each element in a path — `Navigate to **Reports** > **Metrics**`. Bold UI elements the reader acts on rather than quoting them.
- **Internal links** use the page's site path, rooted at the tab — `[Advocate Tiers](/guides/audiences-and-segmentation/advocate-tiers)`. Never a relative `.mdx` path, and never ReadMe's `doc:slug` form (this repo is not on ReadMe; a `doc:` link renders as literal text).
- **Callouts** use Mintlify components — `<Info>`, `<Tip>`, `<Warning>`, `<Danger>`, `<Note>` — not `>` blockquotes and not an emoji-prefixed quote.
- **Images** live in the repo under `images/` and are referenced by root-relative path, either Markdown `![alt](/images/…)` or `<img src="/images/…" />`. Do not add a remote image URL, and never one carrying an `expires=` query parameter.
- **Numbers:** spell out ten and under, numerals for 11 and up (exceptions: ranges, dates, times, measurements, percentages, currencies). One space after a period. No trailing period when a sentence ends in a URL.
- **Keep the page valid MDX.** MDX is JSX-strict: a bare `<`, an unclosed tag, or a stray `{` breaks the build. Run `npx mint@latest validate` before opening the PR.

## Do NOT auto-change (these are not style violations)

- **Literal event and field names, API identifiers, and code:** event names like `promotion clicked`, `share clicked`, `signed up`, `converted`, `shared`, `referred` are real values — keep them verbatim and lowercase. For **UI action instructions**, use "click" ("click the button") in customer-facing web docs; reserve "tap" for in-app placements and mobile SDK. Either way, this UI-verb choice never applies to event names or API fields.
- **Product and report names:** keep the established casing/format for named reports (Events, Metrics, Top Promotion Sources, Incomplete Journey) — check the glossary rather than guessing.
- **`api-reference/*.json`** — the OpenAPI bundles are generated from `extole/extole-specification`; Mintlify renders the whole API reference from them. Don't hand-edit a spec to fix wording.
- Anything the glossary marks as an **open decision / needs a human call** — write it the way the surrounding page already does and surface the inconsistency; do not silently pick a side.

## Guardrails

- **Never invent product behavior, event names, or numbers** to make prose flow. Match the actual system; if unsure, say so and verify (Extole MCP, existing docs).
- A reviewer's suggestion that contains an obvious typo (e.g. "will product" for "will produce") is applied with the typo corrected; a suggestion that is genuinely ambiguous is surfaced to the user, not guessed.
- After content edits, follow the **product-docs-authoring** skill for page structure, `docs.json` nav placement, and the pre-PR self-review checklist.
